1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,000
Hi. In this video, we'll look at using DOMInvader in Burp to test for prototype pollution.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Prototype pollution is a JavaScript vulnerability.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
It enables an attacker to add arbitrary properties to global object prototypes,

4
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,000
which may then be inherited by user-defined objects.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,000
This enables attackers to control object properties that would otherwise be inaccessible.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
You can test for client-side prototype pollution vulnerabilities using DOMInvader.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:40,000
DOMInvader can automatically detect prototype pollution sources and scan for gadgets that you can use to craft an exploit.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:47,000
It can use the prototype pollution sources it discovers to pollute the object.prototype as a proof of concept.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:56,000
If you'd like to follow along with this process, you can use the lab DOMXSS via client-side prototype pollution from our Web Security Academy.

10
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Once the lab has launched, we need to enable prototype pollution detection in DOMInvader.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
In the upper right corner of Burp's browser, we can click on Extensions and then click on Burp's logo.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:17,000
Click on the DOMInvader tab and with DOMInvader enabled, go to the Attack Types section and toggle the switch so prototype pollution is on.

13
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Click Reload to reload the page in the browser and make your changes take effect.

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Now we can start finding potential sources.

15
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:33,000
To do this, right-click in your browser window and select Inspect to open the DevTools panel.

16
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Click the DOMInvader tab and browse around your target site to identify potential sources for prototype pollution.

17
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,000
DOMInvader will display any sources it finds in the sources list.

18
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:55,000
To test the source manually, while on the page in which the source was found, expand the sources list and click on Test.

19
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:59,000
DOMInvader will open the same page in a new tab.

20
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:07,000
In the new tab, open the DevTools panel and select the Console tab.

21
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Expand the Object node to display the object.prototype and confirm that this contains a property called TestProperty.

22
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Now, we'll create a new object in the console.

23
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:39,000
And then use a second console.log command to view the new object.

24
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
This allows us to confirm the new object has inherited TestProperty.

25
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
DOMInvader can also be used to create a proof of concept exploit.

26
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
To do this, we can go back to the original page where we'd been investigating our application.

27
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:02,000
And on the DOMInvader tab, we can select the source we're interested in and click Scan for Gadgets.

28
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,000
DOMInvader will open a new tab and start scanning.

29
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Once the scan has finished, right-click in the new tab and select Inspect.

30
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,000
And select Inspect to open the DevTools panel.

31
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:22,000
Click on the DOMInvader tab and check the contents of the Sinks list.

32
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,000
These are syncs that DOMInvader was able to access via the identified gadgets.

33
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:33,000
Click Exploit next to a sync to test the sync with a proof of concept exploit.

34
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:38,000
Again, DOMInvader opens a new window in which it attempts to call the alert function.

35
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:45,000
If it is able to call the function, then an exploitable prototype pollution vulnerability is confirmed.

